1 | web-frontend | Build the two web surfaces the assistant channel needs, to the signed W0 spec, and nothing else. READ FIRST: docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md on main, which is now signed and is your contract; docs/prd/pages/24-account-concierge.md, the existing settings panel you extend; docs/prd/01-conventions.md for loading, empty and error states; docs/prd/02-shells.md; and the listConciergeBindings and deleteConciergeBinding operations in api.yaml, which already serve Telegram and now also carry assistant bindings after W1-A. THE TWO SURFACES. One, the consent screen that the W1-D authorize endpoint hands off to: login or signup using the existing magic link and Google components, then a consent step that states what the assistant will be able to do and what it will not, with the strings from the spec verbatim. If W1-D has not merged yet, build against its api.yaml contract on branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver and say so in the PR body. Two, the Connected Assistants tile in the settings panel: list each assistant binding by host name and connected-since, a disconnect control per row, and the loading, empty and error triad. The empty copy is from the spec; do not invent one. Every string must exist in the signed spec; if you need one that does not, add it to the spec marked PROPOSED and say so in the PR body rather than inventing it silently. VERIFY, real exit codes captured directly rather than read after a pipe: typecheck, lint, the vitest suite, a production build, and the Voice and Tone lint. Add tests for the triad and for disconnect, and prove the disconnect test fails on the parent commit in a throwaway worktree. Check whether any visual baseline covers the settings panel and report rather than refreshing it blindly. Commit before you break anything; never git stash; never git checkout to revert. Open a PR referencing issue 2799 with Refs and no closing keyword. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. The host names ChatGPT and Claude are permitted in the rendered UI as product content per the signed spec. Do not merge. | feat/ac-w1c-tile

2 | frontend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch feat/ac-w1c-tile. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: does every rendered string trace to the signed spec, and can a user actually disconnect. FIRST, diff every user-visible string in the new components against docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md and report any string that is not in the spec or was added without a PROPOSED marker. SECOND, drive disconnect in a test and confirm it calls the delete binding operation with the right identifier and that the row disappears from the list on success and the error state renders on failure. THIRD, confirm the loading, empty and error triad matches 01-conventions.md and the empty copy is verbatim. FOURTH, confirm the consent screen states both what the assistant can do and what it cannot, per the spec, and that nothing on it collects a passport, date of birth or card. FIFTH, confirm the fail-first claim by checking out the parent and running the disconnect test. SIXTH, typecheck, lint, tests and build pass on the branch; no visual baseline was refreshed blindly. SEVENTH, no long dash, no vendor name in commit or PR text. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC ASSISTANT CHANNEL W1C TILE, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and per-finding file and line citations. No emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | feat/ac-w1c-tile
